st growing  up with all these ideas and and then  challenging them as a woman on an  international stage like Oslo Freedom  Forum when I went back to Saudi Arabia  there was the way there was a fatwa that  I must act I'm not a Muslim anymore and  I was called also traitor the opinion  was so much and the death threats were  real when I was there after I came from  also in 2012 May 2012 and that's the  month I had to go I had to leave the  country I kept going back to Saudi  Arabia but I couldn't live there anymore  and did your father also have to give  you the permission to leave the country  in 2012 yes my father until this day I'm  training for this year and my father  until this day is the one who gives me  the permission to leave the country as a  adult woman a mother of two now before  moving on when exactly is the male  guardianship system and the Saudi law  all women must have a male guardian beat  a father a brother husband or even a son  now let's take a look at what exactly  they used for the woman would need  permission from a male guardian for the  most basic of needs education employment  or even to travel or marriage now  manal you are one of the pioneers of  your generation to demand for the dry  right to drive but you're not alone what  is the fate of your fellow women's  activists in Saudi Arabia so when a few  weeks before the ban is officially  lifted in Saudi Arabia and the woman who  fought all these years for the right to  drive been all summoned all of them in  solitary confinement today and those  women being tortured and these  allegations being confirmed by human  rights organizations and by their  families I confirmed it from their  families myself and those women are  today in jail and no one is really  talking about them no one is bringing  them  and finally they are sending them to  trials after nine months in jail and no  one knows what other charges they're  facing so this is a call to Germany and  today to PN Union to bring up such 